From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: emuTOS-MCD <darekadumon@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 07:30:54 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 136/184] The read path decides on CDD packets that failed
 their checksum

cd_state_action() and cd_prepare() both read cdd_stat[0] with no regard
for whether the packet carrying it checksummed. Every value that switch
can be handed is a decision: 5, B and E throw the sector cache away and
declare the disc gone; 0, 6, 7, 8, D and F abandon the read. Nine of
sixteen nibbles do damage, so noise does damage two times in three.

The guard was already in this file -- cdd_poll() writes cdd_answered
and the disc length only behind `cdd_valid &&` -- and had never been
applied to the one path where the nibble decides anything.

The console names the cost now that the score is published. Round one
reported "drive 7./0": the last packet checksummed, and zero of the
sixteen before it did. That round recorded why nibble 4, the abandon
verdict, on its first attempt. Round two, with the link at 15 of 16,
reported the same state 7 -- and that one is real.

An unreadable packet is not the drive refusing; it is the drive not
having been heard. Keep waiting.
---
 bios/segacd.c | 33 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 29 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/bios/segacd.c b/bios/segacd.c
index 64e8340..8e9ad20 100644
--- a/bios/segacd.c
+++ b/bios/segacd.c
@@ -2115,10 +2115,16 @@ static UBYTE cd_prepare(void)
     for (round = 0; round < 6 && cd_time_left(); round++) {
         UBYTE st = cdd_stat[0];
 
-        if (st == 0x1 || st == 0x4 || st == 0xC)
-            return CD_READY;
-        if (st == 0x5 || st == 0xB || st == 0xE)
-            return CD_NODISC;
+        /* Only a packet that checksummed gets to answer the question.
+         * Same reasoning as cd_state_action(): an invalid nibble
+         * declaring the disc gone is worse than no answer, and an
+         * invalid nibble declaring the drive ready is worse still. */
+        if (cdd_valid) {
+            if (st == 0x1 || st == 0x4 || st == 0xC)
+                return CD_READY;
+            if (st == 0x5 || st == 0xB || st == 0xE)
+                return CD_NODISC;
+        }
 
         cdd_ask_ready();
 
@@ -2154,6 +2160,25 @@ static UBYTE cd_prepare(void)
  * six seconds of silence, three times over, reported as bad luck. */
 static UBYTE cd_state_action(void)
 {
+    /* A packet that failed its checksum has a nibble 0 like any other,
+     * and every value this switch can be handed is a decision: 5, B or
+     * E throws the cache away and declares the disc gone, and six more
+     * abandon the read. Garbage lands in one of those nine two times in
+     * three.
+     *
+     * The guard exists elsewhere in this file already -- cdd_poll()
+     * writes cdd_answered and the disc length only behind `cdd_valid
+     * &&` -- and was never applied to the read path, which is the one
+     * place it decides anything. The console says what that cost: a
+     * round whose CDD score read 0 of the last 16 packets recorded
+     * "why 4", the abandon verdict, on its first attempt.
+     *
+     * An unreadable packet is not the drive refusing. It is the drive
+     * not having been heard, and the answer to that is to keep
+     * waiting. */
+    if (!cdd_valid)
+        return CD_WAIT;
+
     switch (cdd_stat[0]) {
     case 0x1:                   /* the mode variable goes to 3: reading */
     case 0x2:                   /* head moving; position lost, disc known */
